<%#
  The shared "we sent you a message" page body, rendered by delivery A's phone
  verification page and by delivery D's registration pending page. The citizen
  taps the link on the phone; this page never shows the code, but it always
  offers the field to type one (R-D9) for the phone that never got the link.
  A 5-second poll of the status endpoint hands a waiting browser forward the
  moment the phone is proven.

  Two states: while a live verify token still carries the number we can name it
  (masked) and offer the code field; once it has expired there is no number to
  name and nothing a typed code could confirm, so the page says so instead of
  rendering an empty mask.

  Locals — every URL is passed in, because the two pages live at different
  paths and neither may build the other's:
    scope             i18n scope for this page's copy (…phone_verification.show)
    masked_phone      the number as the citizen may see it, never in clear
    expired           no live verify token left
    status_url        the status endpoint the poll asks
    status_method     "get" (A) or "post" (D, which can sign the browser in and
                      is therefore CSRF-protected — see R-D4)
    confirm_url       POST target for the typed code and the reverify number
    resend_url        POST "send again" — always SMS-only (omit to hide)
    change_url        DELETE "change the number" (rendered only where the
                      page's own #number_change_allowed? says it may be used —
                      a page that answers no must own
                      <scope>.expired_lead_start_over, which is the expired
                      lead it gets instead of <scope>.expired_lead)
    check_url         GET this page again
    hidden_fields     name => value pairs every posted form must carry
    reverify          re-verify mode: a grant with no saved phone row
    reverify_store_error / reverify_exhausted   see #enter_reverify_mode!
%>
<%
  reverify = local_assigns.fetch(:reverify, false)
  reverify_store_error = local_assigns.fetch(:reverify_store_error, false)
  reverify_exhausted = local_assigns.fetch(:reverify_exhausted, false)
  hidden_fields = local_assigns.fetch(:hidden_fields, {})
  status_method = local_assigns.fetch(:status_method, "get").to_s
  resend_url = local_assigns.fetch(:resend_url, nil)
%>
<%# With the attempt counter unreachable the limiter is fail-closed and reads
    as exhausted. That is our outage, not the citizen's fault, so the reverify
    page keeps its phone form and says what is wrong instead of leaving them
    with only "I no longer have that number" — see #enter_reverify_mode!. %>
<% reverify_lead = reverify_store_error ? t("#{scope}.reverify_unavailable") : t("#{scope}.reverify_lead") %>
<%# The code entry is always open (R-D9): the citizen who never received the
    link needs it, and a toggle they have to find first is the step at which
    they give up. WhatsApp is named only where it can actually deliver a code —
    the page answers that (PhoneVerificationActions#whatsapp_code_copy?), so
    this shared body never reaches for the provider config itself. %>
<% have_code_key = whatsapp_code_copy? ? "have_code_whatsapp" : "have_code" %>
<%# Same gate, and the same reason spec 5.4.5 gives: do not promise a link the
    message may not carry. Viber and SMS both carry one; WhatsApp's AUTH
    template carries the code alone, so where WhatsApp is in the chain the page
    says "a message" and names the code as the WhatsApp way through. %>
<% sent_to_key = whatsapp_code_copy? ? "sent_to_whatsapp" : "sent_to" %>
<% tap_hint_key = whatsapp_code_copy? ? "tap_hint_whatsapp" : "tap_hint" %>
<%# What an expired page can still offer, and therefore what its lead may say.
    Once the verify token is dead there is no number to name, nothing to resend
    to and nothing a typed code could confirm — the only way forward is a new
    number, and a page that may not take one (delivery D's e-mail variant: the
    address it was sent to is unproven, so pointing the verification at another
    phone would hand the account away) must not be told to enter one. That page
    says start over instead, which is the button it really does have. %>
<% show_change = number_change_allowed? %>
<% expired_lead = show_change ? t("#{scope}.expired_lead") : t("#{scope}.expired_lead_start_over") %>
<%# The reverify page's own status poll reports "expired" too (a grant with no
    saved phone must never redirect, and "expired" is the only existing
    non-redirecting state) — carry the reverify copy into the same data
    attribute the poll's expire() reads, so a poll tick never overwrites it
    with the generic expired copy. %>
<section class="mi-phone-verification" data-mi-phone-verification
         data-mi-reverify="<%= reverify ? "1" : "0" %>"
         data-status-url="<%= status_url %>"
         data-status-method="<%= status_method %>"
         data-expired-text="<%= reverify ? reverify_lead : expired_lead %>">
  <h1><%= t("#{scope}.title") %></h1>
  <p class="mi-phone-verification__lead" data-mi-lead>
    <% if reverify %>
      <%= reverify_lead %>
    <% elsif expired %>
      <%= expired_lead %>
    <% else %>
      <%= t("#{scope}.#{sent_to_key}", phone: masked_phone) %>
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <% if reverify %>
    <%# Reverify takes the phone itself, not a code: the authorization is
        already granted, and #confirm (reused — no new route) matches the
        typed number against that existing grant instead of destroying it.
        Once the AttemptLimiter budget is exhausted, only the secondary
        change-number escape hatch below remains. %>
    <% unless reverify_exhausted %>
      <%= form_with url: confirm_url, method: :post, class: "mi-phone-verification__reverify-form" do %>
        <% hidden_fields.each do |field_name, field_value| %>
          <input type="hidden" name="<%= field_name %>" value="<%= field_value %>">
        <% end %>
        <label for="mi-reverify-phone"><%= t("#{scope}.reverify_phone_label") %></label>
        <input id="mi-reverify-phone" type="tel" name="phone" inputmode="tel" autocomplete="tel" required>
        <button type="submit" class="button button__sm button__secondary">
          <%= t("#{scope}.reverify_submit") %>
        </button>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <% unless expired %>
      <p><%= t("#{scope}.#{tap_hint_key}") %></p>
      <p class="mi-phone-verification__desktop" data-mi-desktop-hint hidden>
        <%= t("#{scope}.desktop_hint") %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <% unless reverify || expired %>
    <%# data-mi-hidden-code is a historical name: this block used to be the
        collapsed "I have a code" toggle. It is open by default now (R-D9);
        the attribute kept its name because the poll script above and the page
        tests both reach for it. %>
    <details class="mi-phone-verification__hidden-code" data-mi-hidden-code open>
      <summary><%= t("#{scope}.#{have_code_key}") %></summary>
      <%# Plain markup on purpose: Decidim's form builder is model-bound and
          raises on a bare url-only form. There is no form object here — one
          field posted straight to #confirm. %>
      <%= form_with url: confirm_url, method: :post do %>
        <% hidden_fields.each do |field_name, field_value| %>
          <input type="hidden" name="<%= field_name %>" value="<%= field_value %>">
        <% end %>
        <label for="mi-verification-code"><%= t("#{scope}.code_label") %></label>
        <input id="mi-verification-code" type="text" name="verification_code"
               inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]{6}" maxlength="6"
               autocomplete="one-time-code" required>
        <button type="submit" class="button button__sm button__secondary">
          <%= t("#{scope}.code_submit") %>
        </button>
      <% end %>
    </details>
  <% end %>

  <%# Resend is offered whenever a number is on record — the newest verify
      token keeps it even after expiry — so the citizen who comes back from a
      reminder e-mail days later can ask for a fresh link (the page passes
      resend_url only when PhoneVerificationActions#phone_on_record has one).
      Reverify is excluded structurally: there is nothing to resend against an
      already-granted row — see PhoneVerificationActions#resend.

      Where the page says the number may NOT be changed (delivery D's e-mail
      variant: the address it was sent to is unproven, so pointing the
      verification at another phone would hand the account away), that button
      is not rendered at all — it could only ever produce a refusal. On expiry
      that page offers resend and start over, and its lead
      (<scope>.expired_lead_start_over, chosen above) says exactly that. %>
  <div class="mi-phone-verification__actions">
    <% if !reverify && resend_url.present? %>
      <span class="mi-phone-verification__resend" data-mi-resend>
        <%= button_to t("#{scope}.resend"), resend_url, method: :post, class: "button button__sm button__secondary" %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
    <% if show_change %>
      <%= button_to(
            reverify ? t("#{scope}.reverify_change") : t("#{scope}.change_number"),
            change_url,
            method: :delete,
            class: "button button__sm #{(!reverify && expired) ? "button__secondary" : "button__text"}",
            data: { "mi-change" => "" }
          ) %>
    <% end %>
    <%= link_to t("#{scope}.check"), check_url, class: "button button__sm button__text" %>
  </div>


</section>

<script>
  (function () {
    var root = document.querySelector("[data-mi-phone-verification]");
    if (!root) return;
    var hint = root.querySelector("[data-mi-desktop-hint]");
    if (hint && !/Mobi|Android/i.test(navigator.userAgent)) hint.hidden = false;

    var url = root.getAttribute("data-status-url");
    // A page whose status endpoint can sign this browser in must not offer
    // that as a GET, so the method is per-page and a non-GET poll carries the
    // CSRF token Rails will demand.
    var method = (root.getAttribute("data-status-method") || "GET").toUpperCase();
    // Twelve polls a minute against a per-session budget of thirty: enough
    // headroom for the same citizen to have the page open twice, or for a
    // reload to overlap the previous tab, without either being throttled.
    var BASE_INTERVAL_MS = 5000;
    var MAX_INTERVAL_MS = 60000;
    var interval = BASE_INTERVAL_MS;
    var timer = null;
    var done = false;

    function requestOptions() {
      var headers = { "Accept": "application/json" };
      if (method !== "GET") {
        var meta = document.querySelector("meta[name=csrf-token]");
        if (meta) headers["X-CSRF-Token"] = meta.getAttribute("content");
      }
      return { method: method, credentials: "same-origin", headers: headers };
    }

    // setTimeout, not setInterval: an interval cannot slow down, and the
    // back-off below is the whole point.
    function stop() { if (timer) { clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; } }

    function finish() { done = true; stop(); }

    // A 429 says this session is asking faster than it is allowed to —
    // maybe because of another tab. Doubling up to a minute stops the poll
    // from spending its whole budget on rejections, and it keeps waiting:
    // the citizen may still be about to tap the link.
    function backOff() { interval = Math.min(interval * 2, MAX_INTERVAL_MS); }

    // Server-rendered expired state, reached without a reload.
    function expire() {
      finish();
      var lead = root.querySelector("[data-mi-lead]");
      var text = root.getAttribute("data-expired-text");
      if (lead && text) lead.textContent = text;
      var code = root.querySelector("[data-mi-hidden-code]");
      if (code) code.hidden = true;
      // No live link left to resend against, on either variant.
      var resend = root.querySelector("[data-mi-resend]");
      if (resend) resend.hidden = true;
      // A page whose way forward is start over ([data-mi-start-over]) has no
      // change-number button to promote, and data-expired-text above already
      // names start over. Keying on THAT rather than on the change button's
      // absence is what keeps this tick and the server-rendered expired page
      // saying the same thing.
      var startOver = document.querySelector("[data-mi-start-over]");
      // Reverify already styles change-number as its secondary action (the
      // phone-input form is the primary one); the poll's own "expired" tick
      // (reverify always reports it — see PhoneVerificationActions#status)
      // must not re-promote it to look primary.
      var change = root.querySelector("[data-mi-change]");
      if (change && !startOver && root.getAttribute("data-mi-reverify") !== "1") change.className = "button button__sm button__secondary";
    }

    function poll() {
      fetch(url, requestOptions())
        .then(function (r) {
          if (r.status === 401 || r.status === 403) { finish(); return null; }
          if (r.status === 429) { backOff(); return null; }
          // A 5xx is the server having a bad minute, not the end of the wait:
          // the citizen may still be about to tap the link, so keep asking and
          // keep the numbers already on screen.
          if (!r.ok) return null;
          // fetch follows the sign-in redirect for us, so a 200 that is not
          // JSON means the session is gone and there is nothing left to poll.
          var type = r.headers.get("content-type") || "";
          if (type.indexOf("json") === -1) { finish(); return null; }
          // An answer we could read means we are back inside the budget.
          interval = BASE_INTERVAL_MS;
          return r.json();
        })
        .then(function (data) {
          if (data) {
            if (data.state === "verified" && data.next) { finish(); window.location.assign(data.next); }
            else if (data.state === "expired") { expire(); }
          }
          start();
        })
        // Re-armed in both branches rather than in a .finally, which some
        // browsers that have fetch still do not have.
        .catch(function () { start(); });
    }

    function tick() { timer = null; poll(); }

    // The visibility check belongs here, not only in the visibilitychange
    // handler: a fetch that resolves after the tab was hidden re-arms the
    // chain through this same function, and a backgrounded tab that keeps
    // polling is exactly what the handler is trying to stop.
    function start() { if (!done && !timer && !document.hidden) timer = setTimeout(tick, interval); }

    // A backgrounded tab does not need to ask every five seconds.
    document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", function () {
      if (document.hidden) { stop(); } else { start(); }
    });
    if (!document.hidden) start();
  })();
</script>
